{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016k7THSZh33teUG9tWpc5CU","type":"tool_result","content":"# MuJS C -> Rust translation conventions (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n\nWe are translating the MuJS C library in `c_src/` into the Rust cdylib in\n`translation/`. The Rust library must be a *mechanical, behaviour identical*\ntranslation: same algorithms, same order of checks, same output bytes, same\nbugs. Do NOT improve, refactor, modernise or \"fix\" anything.\n\n## Ground rules\n\n* Work only on the file you were assigned. NEVER modify any other file in\n  `translation/src/`, and NEVER modify anything in `c_src/`.\n* The crate is one Rust crate; every C file becomes one Rust module.\n* Everything is `unsafe` raw pointer code. Do not use `String`, `Vec`, `Box`,\n  `format!`, `println!`, iterators, or any Rust std allocation. Use only the\n  libc bindings and the helpers already declared in `src/jsi.rs`.\n* Never print with Rust macros. Use the libc `printf`/`putchar`/`fputs`/\n  `snprintf`/`sprintf`/`fprintf` bindings from `src/jsi.rs` so output bytes and\n  buffering match the C library exactly.\n* Integer arithmetic must wrap like C: the crate is built with\n  `overflow-checks = false`, but where the C code clearly relies on wrapping or\n  on shifts >= bit width, use `wrapping_*` methods.\n\n## Module skeleton\n\n```rust\n//! Translation of <name>.c\n\nuse crate::*;            // types, constants, libc bindings, other modules\n\n/* ... translated code ... */\n```\n\n`use crate::*;` gives you: everything from `src/jsi.rs` (all types, constants,\nlibc `extern \"C\"` bindings, helper functions) plus every public function of\nevery other module. Macros (`cs!`, `js_typeerror!`, `js_try!`, `vol!`, ...) are\navailable everywhere automatically (`#[macro_use] mod jsi`).\n\nIf you need a libc function that is not declared yet, add your own\n`extern \"C\" { ... }` block at the top of your file (do not edit jsi.rs).\n\n## Naming / linkage\n\n* A C function that is NOT `static` becomes\n  ```rust\n  #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n  pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn name(args) -> ret { ... }\n  ```\n  Its exported symbol name must match the C linker name exactly.\n* A C `static` function becomes a private `unsafe fn name(...)`. Do not put\n  `#[no_mangle]` or `pub` on it (private avoids name clashes between modules).\n* The stub file you are replacing already contains the exact signatures of the\n  non-static functions of your C file. Keep those signatures byte for byte.\n* Functions used as `js_CFunction` callbacks must be\n  `unsafe extern \"C\" fn(J: *mut js_State)` and are passed as `Some(name)`.\n\n## Type mapping\n\n| C | Rust |\n|---|---|\n| `int`, `unsigned`, `short`, `char`, `double` | `c_int`, `c_uint`, `c_short`, `c_char`, `f64` |\n| `char *` / `const char *` | `*mut c_char` / `*const c_char` |\n| `void *` | `*mut c_void` |\n| `NULL` | `null()` / `null_mut()` |\n| `js_State *J` | `*mut js_State` |\n| struct field named `type` | field named `type_` |\n| `enum js_Class` / `enum js_Type` field | `c_int` with the `JS_C*` / `JS_T*` constants |\n\nAll structs are declared in `src/jsi.rs` with `#[repr(C)]` and identical\nlayout. Read that file before starting.\n\n### js_Value\n\n`js_Value` is a `#[repr(C)]` union. Use the helpers from jsi.rs:\n\n```rust\nvtype(v)                  /* v->t.type as c_int */\nsetvtype(v, JS_TNUMBER)   /* v->t.type = JS_TNUMBER */\n(*v).u.number             /* v->u.number */\n(*v).u.boolean, (*v).u.litstr, (*v).u.memstr, (*v).u.object\nshrstrp(v)                /* char* to v->u.shrstr */\nstrp(memstr)              /* char* to js_String->p */\njsv_isstring(v)           /* JSV_ISSTRING(v) */\njsv_tostring_raw(v)       /* JSV_TOSTRING(v) */\nOFF_VALUE_TYPE            /* soffsetof(js_Value, t.type) == 15 */\n```\n\nFlexible array members (`js_Property.name`, `js_Iterator.name`,\n`js_String.p`, `js_StringNode.string`) are `[c_char; 1]` fields; get a pointer\nwith `propname(p)`, `itername(p)`, `strp(s)`, `nodestring(n)`, and allocate\nwith the `OFF_*` offset constants (e.g. `OFF_PROPERTY_NAME + n`).\n\n## String literals\n\n`cs!(\"hello\")` produces a NUL terminated `*const c_char`. Use it for every C\nstring literal, including format strings.\n\n## Errors and exceptions\n\nThe `js_*error` functions are variadic in C. Use these macros, which format\ninto a 256 byte buffer with libc `snprintf` exactly like the C originals and\nthen throw:\n\n```rust\njs_error!(J, \"stack underflow!\");\njs_typeerror!(J, \"'%s' is read-only\", name);\njs_rangeerror!(J, \"invalid array length\");\njs_referenceerror!(J, \"'%s' is not defined\", str);\njs_syntaxerror!(J, \"...\", ...);\njs_evalerror!(J, \"...\");\njs_urierror!(J, \"...\");\n```\n\nThey diverge (`-> !`), so no `return` is needed after them. `js_throw(J)` also\ndiverges.\n\nIf your C file has its own `static` variadic error helper (for example\n`jsY_error` in jslex.c), translate it as a non-variadic\n`unsafe fn jsY_error_str(J: *mut js_State, msg: *const c_char) -> !` plus a\nlocal macro that formats with `snprintf` into a buffer **of exactly the same\nsize as the C original** and calls it:\n\n```rust\nmacro_rules! jsY_error {\n    ($J:expr, $fmt:expr $(, $a:expr)*) => {{\n        let mut __b: [c_char; 512] = [0; 512];\n        snprintf(__b.as_mut_ptr(), 512, cs!($fmt) $(, $a)*);\n        jsY_error_str($J, __b.as_ptr())\n    }};\n}\n```\n\n`jsC_error(J, node, fmt, ...)` already exists in `src/varargs.rs`; from Rust\ncode call `crate::varargs::jsC_error_str(J, node, msg)` through a local macro\nin the same style (buffer size 256, as in the C `msgbuf`).\n\n## try/catch (setjmp/longjmp)\n\n```rust\nif js_try!(J) != 0 {\n    /* exception path */\n    js_throw(J);\n}\n/* body */\njs_endtry(J);\n```\n\n`js_try!` MUST be written directly in the function whose frame the exception\nreturns to (never inside a helper function or closure).\n\n**Important:** any local variable that is assigned inside the `try` body and\nread in the exception path must be accessed with the volatile helpers, exactly\nwhere the C source used the `volatile` qualifier:\n\n```rust\nlet mut out: *mut c_char = null_mut();\nif js_try!(J) != 0 {\n    js_free(J, vol!(out) as *mut c_void);\n    js_throw(J);\n}\nsetvol!(out, js_malloc(J, n) as *mut c_char);\n... use vol!(out) ...\njs_endtry(J);\njs_free(J, vol!(out) as *mut c_void);\n```\n\n## Control flow translation\n\n* `goto label;` inside a function: use a labelled block\n  ```rust\n  'body: { ... break 'body; ... }   /* code after the block == label target */\n  ```\n  or a boolean flag plus restructuring. Keep the semantics identical.\n* C `switch` with fallthrough: replicate the fallthrough explicitly.\n* `for (a; b; c) { ... continue; }` -> `while` loop, but remember `continue`\n  must still execute the increment `c`.\n* `while (x--)`, `while (--x)` etc: translate the pre/post semantics carefully.\n* Do not use `match` on constants unless every arm is a `pub const` (they are).\n\n## Static (file scope) variables\n\n```rust\nstatic mut FOO: c_int = 0;              /* mutable C static */\nstatic TABLE: [c_int; 3] = [1, 2, 3];   /* const C static */\n```\nFor arrays of string pointers use `[&str; N]` with `\"...\\0\"` entries and take\n`.as_ptr() as *const c_char` (raw pointers are not `Sync` so they cannot be in\na plain `static`). Self-referential statics (like the AA-tree sentinels) are\ninitialised lazily; see `src/jsproperty.rs` for the pattern.\n\n## Examples already finished (read them for style)\n\n`src/jsvalue.rs`, `src/jsrun.rs`, `src/jsproperty.rs`, `src/jsgc.rs`,\n`src/jsintern.rs`, `src/jsstate.rs`, `src/jserror.rs`, `src/utf.rs`.\n\n## Verifying\n\nFrom `translation/`:\n\n```\ncargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -A8 \"src/<yourfile>.rs\"\n```\n\nOther stub modules are still unimplemented, so ignore errors that are not in\nyour own file. Keep iterating until your file has no errors and no warnings\nother than dead-code ones. Do not stop with a partially translated file: every\nfunction of your C file must be present.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018kW2aRAMNFNM6KCEPPBgpx","session_id":"2be16944-0c16-4bac-97a9-ffb5f1a29386","uuid":"83e3daff-f025-42a2-a701-a7a651239f6a","timestamp":"2026-09-04T05:00:03.562Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Phase C syntax error tests"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_dgxy72nmz5g77r6yswwrtypx4bcbllhhofrowlhi76dtubkrt4va","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01FqLgu3rFhCxemNG91kaU9i","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Phase C runtime error tests","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"You are writing DIFFERENTIAL tests for a C→Rust translation of MuJS.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-oXxm9Q\n- `c_src/` = original C (ground truth, NEVER modify, never rebuild)\n- `translation/` = Rust crate under test. Run all cargo commands from `translation/`.\n\nYOUR TASK: create exactly ONE new file, `translation/tests/phase_c_runtime.rs`, with\ndifferential error-path tests for rows 170..338 of `translation/ERRORS.md`\n(jsrun.c / jsstate.c / jsvalue.c / jsproperty.c / jsintern.c / jserror.c / jsgc.c).\nRead those rows first.\n\nSTRICT RULES\n- Create ONLY `translation/tests/phase_c_runtime.rs`. Do NOT modify anything else:\n  not c_src/, not translation/src/, not tests/common/mod.rs, not other test files.\n- Never call Rust functions directly — only the two `.so` exports, via the harness.\n\nHARNESS (read `translation/tests/common/mod.rs` and `translation/tests/phase_b_api.rs`\nfor working examples). Start your file with:\n    #![allow(non_snake_case)]\n    mod common;\n    use common::*;\nAvailable:\n- `diff_eval(label, src, flags)` — runs JS source in both libraries, comparing\n  `\"load-error(rc) msg\"` / `\"throw(rc) msg\"` / `\"ok <repr>\"` byte for byte.\n- `diff_native(label, act, flags)` with `act: fn(&Api, JS)` — drives the RAW C API\n  inside a `js_pcall`, so a thrown error is caught and rendered instead of killing\n  the process. Record observations with `emit(&str)`; pass parameters through\n  `set_pi/pi/pic`, `set_ps/ps`, `set_pf/pf`.\n- `repr_at(a, J, idx)` / `str_at(a, J, idx)` — NON-destructive observation. Never\n  observe with raw `js_torepr`/`js_tostring`: they overwrite the stack slot.\n- `Api` has a field per exported symbol; `libs()`, `same()`, `Rng`, `capture_stdout`,\n  `mask_ptrs`.\n\nIMPORTANT — process-fatal paths are ALREADY covered elsewhere. Do NOT write tests\nthat make an error escape to `js_throw` with no enclosing try (that aborts the whole\ntest process): always go through `diff_eval` or `diff_native`. Do NOT use\n`diff_isolated` / `isolated_child` (owned by another file). If a row can ONLY be\nreached fatally, list it in your final report instead.\n\nTEST CONTENT — cover EVERY row 170..338:\n- js_pop/js_remove/js_replace stack underflow messages; `js_rot` counts.\n- `CHECKSTACK` stack-overflow (\"stack overflow\") from deeply nested pushes inside a\n  cfunction and from JS recursion; JS_ENVLIMIT (\"too much recursion\"), JS_TRYLIMIT.\n- readonly / dontconf paths of jsR_setproperty, jsR_defproperty, jsR_delproperty in\n  BOTH strict and non-strict mode, for: plain properties, array `length`, String\n  `length` and indices, RegExp `source`/`global`/`ignoreCase`/`multiline`,\n  getter-only accessors, non-extensible/sealed/frozen objects, transient (primitive)\n  receivers.\n- `invalid array length` / `array too large` (JS_ARRAYLIMIT = 1<<26) via both the\n  `length` setter and `jsR_setarrayindex`.\n- JS_STRLIMIT (1<<28) string concat range errors — if not reachable within memory,\n  say so in the report rather than allocating gigabytes.\n- Every jserror.c thrower: js_error/js_evalerror/js_rangeerror/js_referenceerror/\n  js_syntaxerror/js_typeerror/js_urierror and the js_new*error constructors, called\n  directly through the Api inside `diff_native`, including message truncation at the\n  256-byte buffer boundary (build a 300+ char message).\n- `js_toobject`/`jsV_toobject` on undefined and null (\"cannot convert undefined to\n  object\", \"cannot convert null to object\"); js_toregexp on a non-regexp;\n  js_touserdata with a wrong tag; js_iscallable/js_call on non-callables\n  (\"... is not callable\"); js_instanceof with non-object/non-callable right operand.\n- Referenceerror paths: reading an undeclared variable, assigning in strict mode,\n  `delete` of an unresolvable reference.\n- `js_setlimit` runlimit (\"excessive CPU usage\") and memlimit (\"out of memory\")\n  behaviour, using small limits and a loop.\n- Out-of-range enum values across the FFI boundary: `js_toprimitive` hint values\n  (-1, 0, 4, 99), `js_newstate` flags (2, -1, 0x7fffffff), property attribute values\n  (8, 255, -1), regexp flag values (8, 255, -1) to `js_newregexp`, and\n  `jsV_newobject` class values (16, 99, -1) — assert both libraries agree.\n- Null pointer arguments where the C would accept them: e.g. `js_regexec(prog, s,\n  NULL, 0)`, `js_setreport(J, NULL)`, `js_atpanic(J, NULL)`, `js_newcfunction` with a\n  NULL function pointer that is never called, `js_newuserdatax` with NULL callbacks.\n- Randomized coverage (fixed seed): several hundred random (operation, index, value)\n  triples through the property/index API where the indices stay inside the frame,\n  asserting identical results.\n\nWORKFLOW\n1. Write the file.\n2. `cd translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test phase_c_runtime -- --test-threads=1`\n   (always `--offline`).\n3. Iterate until it compiles and runs. If the process crashes (SIGSEGV/SIGABRT), the\n   test you just added drove the library into a fatal path: restructure it so the\n   error is caught, or drop that row and report it.\n4. If a test reveals a GENUINE divergence between the C and Rust `.so`, do NOT touch\n   translation/src — mark the test `#[ignore = \"DIVERGENCE: ...\"]` with a precise\n   comment and continue. The file must end with all non-ignored tests PASSING.\n5. Write covered row numbers to `tmpwk/covered_runtime.txt` (`<row> <test fn>`).\n\nFINAL REPORT: number of test fns, rows covered, rows you could not cover (and why),\nand FULL detail of every divergence found (exact input, C output, Rust output)."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":410,"cache_read_input_tokens":213318,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":410,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2be16944-0c16-4bac-97a9-ffb5f1a29386","uuid":"84346ed9-2aee-41b2-b3b6-203f239f4db9","timestamp":"2026-09-04T05:00:06.114Z"}
