RI rows |\n\nAll 161 rows 339..499 are covered. Every source runs under both `flags = 0` and `flags = JS_STRICT`. Only `diff_eval` and `diff_native` are used (no `diff_isolated`, no direct Rust calls).\n\n## Divergences found: NONE\n\nEvery case matched byte for byte between `c_src/build/libmujs.so` and `translation/target/release/libmujs.so`.\n\n## Anti-vacuity verification\n\nBecause a source that fails to parse would make both libraries agree trivially, I independently spot-checked ~75 representative cases against the C `.so` via `ctypes` and confirmed each reaches the documented error site, e.g.:\n\n- `Object.getPrototypeOf(1)` → `throw(1) TypeError: not an object`\n- `Object.defineProperty({},'x',{value:1,get:function(){}})` → `TypeError: value/writable and get/set attributes are exclusive`\n- `new Array(-1)` → `RangeError: invalid array length`; `new Array(134217728)` → `RangeError: array too large`\n- `Array.prototype.sort.call({length:1e300,...}, cmp)` → `RangeError: array is too large to sort`\n- `new Array(3).reduce(f)` → `TypeError: no initial value`; `Array.prototype.toString.call(null)` → `TypeError: 'this' is not an object`\n- `(255).toString(37)` → `RangeError: invalid radix`; `(5).toFixed(101)` → `RangeError: precision 101 out of range`\n- `new Array(6000).join('a').search(/a*b/)` → `Error: regexec failed` (REG_MAXREC row 406)\n- `new Date(NaN).toISOString()` → `RangeError: invalid date`; `Date.prototype.toJSON.call({toISOString:1})` → `TypeError: this.toISOString is not a function`\n- `JSON.parse(\"{\\\"a\\\" 1}\")` → `SyntaxError: JSON: unexpected token: (number) (expected ':')`; `JSON.parse(\"'a'\")` → `SyntaxError: JSON:1: unexpected character: '''`\n- `var a={}; a.a=a; JSON.stringify(a)` → `TypeError: cyclic object value`\n- `decodeURI('%')` → `URIError: truncated escape sequence`; `decodeURI('%zz')` → `URIError: invalid escape sequence`\n- The three JS_STRLIMIT (1<<28) rows genuinely fire: `for(i=0;i<27;++i)s=s+s; [s,s,s].join('')` and `s.concat(s)` and (2^28 base) `s.concat('x')` all → `RangeError: invalid string length`\n\n## Notes on rows that cannot be driven to their error state from JS\n\nDocumented inline in the file and in the coverage map; they are still exercised on their success paths:\n\n- **387, 388** (`callbound`/`constructbound` with a negative `__BoundArguments__.length`): that property is defined `JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF`, so JS cannot make the length negative. The test attempts both plain assignment and `Object.defineProperty` (both compared differentially) and then exercises bound call/construct with 0/1/N bound args.\n- **448** (`parseDateTime` ms outside 0..999): dead check in C — `toint` consumes exactly 3 digits. Exercised via `.1`, `.123`, `.1234` fractional-second inputs.\n- **482** (unbounded recursion in `jsonvalue`): the actual C-stack overflow aborts the process, so only deep-but-legal nesting (depths 1, 2, 8, 64, 200, arrays and objects) is compared.\n- **498** (`js_repr` with `sb == NULL`): dead path in C — `js_putc(J,&sb,0)` always allocates. `js_repr` itself is exercised through `diff_native` on cyclic, deep, and primitive values.\n- `Math.random()` and 0-argument `new Date()`/`Date.now()` are inherently nondeterministic, so only their contracts (type, range, finiteness) are compared, not their values.","usage":{"total_tokens":124114,"tool_uses":27,"duration_ms":726987},"uuid":"428a5f01-8708-473c-8e33-fc8733d75f8b","session_id":"2be16944-0c16-4bac-97a9-ffb5f1a29386"}
